IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `Singapore miles-card reward programmes — the shared domain`

-- ===========================================================================
-- THE SHARED ONTOLOGY for eight Singapore consumer card reward programmes,
-- encoded from the issuers' own terms and conditions (private contract text,
-- NOT law, and nothing in this corpus is advice about anyone's real cover).
--
-- WHY ONE ONTOLOGY. Every card answers the same question -- "what does THIS
-- transaction earn on THIS card?" -- over the same facts: who the merchant
-- is, how the cardholder paid, what the merchant's acquirer reported, and
-- where the month's caps stand. Eight private notions of "a transaction"
-- would let the eight answers disagree about what was asked.
--
-- So this module owns the nouns. Each card module owns its own rules,
-- imports these types, and never redeclares them.
--
-- MEMBERSHIP TEST. A field belongs here iff a witness of ordinary competence
-- could depose to it WITHOUT reading any T&C: "I tapped my phone at a
-- terminal", "the statement shows MCC 5812", "I have linked my yuu account".
-- Whether that tap earns 4 mpd or 0.4 is a rule, and lives in the card
-- module for the clause that says so.
--
-- TWO AXES, NOT ONE (spec section 4.4). "Contactless" is overloaded and is
-- the wrong primitive. The cardholder controls the CREDENTIAL (the physical
-- card, or a wallet token); the merchant's acquirer controls the INDICATOR
-- the bank receives (online vs card-present). DBS Woman's Card T&C cl.13:
-- "DBS determines an online transaction based on system indicators. These
-- indicators are decided by the merchant and their banker". Citi 10X cl.6(ii):
-- "These indicators are decided by the relevant merchant and/or their acquirer
-- and are not determined by Citibank". HSBC Revolution cl.6: "based on system
-- indicators which are determined by the merchants and the merchant's
-- acquiring bank". Three issuers, one design: the indicator is a fact about
-- the MERCHANT, latent to the cardholder at decision time, so it carries an
-- explicit `Unknown` and every rule that reads it answers conditionally.
--
-- CATALA. This corpus must lower to Catala (spec section 2). DECLARE-only
-- here; no rule anywhere reads a section GIVEN; every rule takes its inputs
-- positionally.
-- ===========================================================================


§§ `The cards`

-- The eight programmes in the household stack. DBS yuu is issued on two
-- networks and the T&C treats them differently at named merchants (Appendix 1:
-- "Points will not be awarded for any transactions made with DBS yuu American
-- Express Card" at 7-Eleven before 13 Aug 2026 and at Charge+), so the network
-- is part of the card's identity rather than a separate fact.
DECLARE Card IS ONE OF
    `DBS yuu Visa`
    `DBS yuu American Express`
    `DBS Woman's World`
    `UOB Lady's Solitaire`
    `HSBC Revolution`
    `Citi Rewards`
    `POSB PAssion Debit`
    `UOB PRVI Miles`
    `Citi PremierMiles`

-- UOB Lady's Cards T&C Schedule 1: the transaction categories a Solitaire
-- cardmember elects two of (cl.4(b)). Each is defined by an MCC list in the
-- Schedule; the mapping from MCC to category is the UOB module's rule.
DECLARE `Solitaire category` IS ONE OF
    `Beauty & Wellness`
    Dining
    Entertainment
    Family
    Fashion
    Transport
    Travel


§§ `How the cardholder paid`

-- What the cardholder can see themselves doing at the moment of payment.
-- This is NOT the channel the bank sees; see `Merchant indicator` below.
DECLARE `Payment channel` IS ONE OF
    `Tapped at a terminal`
    `Inserted or swiped at a terminal`
    `Paid in a website or app`
    `Card on file or recurring billing`

-- What was presented. A wallet token is Apple Pay, Google Pay or Samsung Pay;
-- HSBC Revolution cl.5 names all three. `Via Amaze` is a third-party proxy
-- card (Instarem Amaze) that forwards the charge to the underlying card; it
-- appears in the household's routing and in several exclusion lists, so it is
-- a credential the cardholder can depose to.
DECLARE Credential IS ONE OF
    `The physical card`
    `A mobile wallet token`
    `Via Amaze`

-- The system indicator the merchant's acquirer attaches to the transaction
-- when it is posted. Three issuers say in terms that they do not determine
-- it. It is latent but stable per merchant (spec section 8), so a cardholder
-- who has seen this merchant post before can supply it; one who has not
-- supplies `Unknown`, and the rules answer under both branches rather than
-- defaulting either way.
DECLARE `Merchant indicator` IS ONE OF
    `Flags online`
    `Flags card-present`
    Unknown


§§ `Merchants and statement descriptions the documents name`

-- CATALA HAS NO STRINGS, AND THE T&Cs KEY MERCHANTS BY NAME (measured
-- 2026-09-21; see NOTES.md section 3). So the string work is done once, in
-- layer 1 (`classify.l4`, which reads `merchant name` and `transaction
-- description` with the prefix builtin `CONTAINS`), and every rule reads only
-- the closed enumerations below. A witness can depose to "it was Cold
-- Storage" as readily as to the printed name, so the membership test holds.
--
-- The constructors are the merchants some held document NAMES. A merchant no
-- document names is `Another merchant` -- the household app's categories
-- (Shopee, Netflix, Tesla ...) live in `categorize.l4`, not here.
DECLARE Merchant IS ONE OF
    `Cold Storage`             -- yuu cl.8: Cold Storage, CS Fresh, Jasons Deli count as ONE; PAssion cl.5(i)
    `Cold Storage On The Go`   -- yuu Appendix 1: within Esso stations, base only
    Giant                      -- yuu cl.8: Giant, Giant Hypermart, Giant Express; PAssion cl.5(i)
    Guardian                   -- yuu cl.8; PAssion cl.5(i)
    `7-Eleven`                 -- yuu cl.8; PAssion cl.5(i)
    foodpanda                  -- yuu cl.8: food delivery, panda mart, panda shops; PAssion cl.5(vi)
    Gojek                      -- yuu cl.8; PAssion cl.5(v)
    SimplyGo                   -- yuu cl.8: bus and train rides via SimplyGo
    `Charge+`                  -- yuu cl.8
    CHAGEE                     -- yuu cl.8: orders placed via the CHAGEE app only
    Singtel                    -- yuu cl.8: Singtel Shop, Singtel Exclusive Retailers; PAssion cl.5(iv)
    BreadTalk                  -- PAssion cl.5(ii)
    `Toast Box`                -- PAssion cl.5(ii)
    `Thye Moh Chan`            -- PAssion cl.5(ii)
    `Butter Bean`              -- PAssion cl.5(ii)
    `Food Republic`            -- PAssion cl.5(ii): withdrawn wef 27 Oct 2024
    `Food Junction`            -- PAssion cl.5(ii): withdrawn wef 27 Oct 2024
    `Mandai Wildlife Group`    -- PAssion cl.5(iii): Zoo, River Wonders, Night Safari, Bird Paradise
    `Another merchant`

-- The families of statement-line text the exclusion lists match on: DBS
-- Woman's cl.11 Table 2, yuu cl.9(i), Citi exclusion list paragraph 3, UOB
-- cl.35, HSBC cl.4. A transaction may match several (an "AMAZE*AXS" line
-- matches two), so `Transaction` carries a LIST of them. Layer 1 decides
-- membership by substring, which is what "matching the terms" and "any
-- combination, permutation or derivation" describe; the rules then test
-- `elem`. Read the issuer's own list to see which family each rule uses.
DECLARE `Description pattern` IS ONE OF
    AXS                        -- AXS, AXS BizPay, AXS PTE LTD, PAYPAL AXS PTE LTD
    AMAZE                      -- AMAZE, AMAZE*<merchant>
    `EZ-Link`                  -- EZLINK, EZ-LINK, EZ Link, MYEZLINK
    TRANSIT                    -- TRANSIT, TRANSIT 3, Transit Link
    `BUS/MRT`                  -- BUS/MRT (Citi paragraph 3)
    `SimplyGo description`     -- SIMPLYGO* (Citi paragraph 3); distinct from the SimplyGo merchant
    FlashPay                   -- FLASHPAY, NETSFLASHPAY, NETS VCASHCARD
    YouTrip                    -- YOUTRIP, Youtrip Singapore SG
    CardUp                     -- CARDUP
    iPaymy                     -- IPAYMY
    FavePay                    -- FAVEPAY, FAVE ECARD
    ShopeePay                  -- SHOPEEPAY
    SAM                        -- SAM PAYMENTS, SINGPOST-SAM, "SAM –"
    RazerPay                   -- RAZERPAY
    SmoovPay                   -- SmoovPay, SMOOVPAY (EC), SMOOV PTE LTD
    `Singtel Dash`             -- SINGTEL DASH
    PayPal                     -- PAYPAL (yuu/PAssion Appendix 1; HSBC cl.4)
    `Skrill or Moneybookers`   -- SKR*SKRILL.COM, MB MONEYBOOKERS.COM
    `Forex or CFD platform`    -- OANDA, SAXO CAP MKTS, IGMARKETS, PLUS500, CITY INDEX, BANC DE BINARY, SKYFX, XTRADE, TOROPTION
    `Cryptocurrency platform`  -- COINBASE, BITSTAMP, BITPANDA, COINIFY, INDACOIN, SIMPLEX_*, and the rest of Woman's Table 2
    `Payment platform`         -- BAGUS, CANTINE, SEDAP, SGEBIZ, Singapore E-Business, KASHMI, MATCHMOVE PAY, IPLMILESLIFE
    `Government levy`          -- RWS-LEVY (UOB cl.35)

-- What KIND of posting this is. Every issuer excludes the same family of
-- non-purchases -- fees, interest, instalment plans, transfers, cash
-- advances, bill payments -- and none of them is visible in an MCC or a
-- merchant name: they are attributes of the posting, which the statement
-- does show. Citi cl.5(i)-(iii), yuu cl.9(a)-(e), UOB cl.32, HSBC cl.4,
-- DBS Woman's cl.11(iii)-(vii).
DECLARE `Charge kind` IS ONE OF
    `Retail purchase`
    `Annual fee or bank charge`
    `Interest or finance charge`
    `Instalment plan transaction`
    `Balance or funds transfer`
    `Cash advance or quasi-cash`
    `Bill payment via internet banking`
    `Telephone or mail order`
    `Pre-authorisation`
    `Refund or reversal`


§§ `The transaction`

-- One charge, as the cardholder and the statement describe it. `merchant
-- category code` is the acquirer's MCC as it appears on the statement (every
-- issuer here says the acquirer assigns it and the issuer does not).
-- `merchant name` and `transaction description` are the printed strings;
-- `merchant` and `description patterns` are their classification by layer 1
-- (`classify.l4`), and are what the rules read -- a rule never compares a
-- string (Catala has none). `charge kind` is the posting's kind. `posted in a
-- foreign currency` is the fact DBS Woman's cl.12, PAssion cl.7(d) and the flat
-- cards' overseas rates turn on, as a boolean because a rule may not compare
-- the `currency` string. `posting date` may lag `transaction
-- date`; DBS attributes to the transaction date and UOB to the posting date,
-- and both are facts the statement shows.
DECLARE Transaction
    HAS `merchant name`             IS A STRING
        `merchant`                  IS A Merchant
        `merchant category code`    IS A NUMBER
        `transaction description`   IS A STRING
        `description patterns`      IS A LIST OF `Description pattern`
        `charge kind`               IS A `Charge kind`
        `amount`                    IS A NUMBER
        `currency`                  IS A STRING
        `posted in a foreign currency` IS A BOOLEAN
        `transaction date`          IS A DATE
        `posting date`              IS A DATE
        `payment channel`           IS A `Payment channel`
        `credential`                IS A Credential
        `merchant indicator`        IS A `Merchant indicator`
        `made in Singapore`         IS A BOOLEAN


§§ `The cardholder`

-- Standing facts about the cardholder that the T&Cs make operative and that
-- change rarely: which Solitaire categories are elected this Quarter (UOB
-- cl.4(b), cl.5); whether the yuu account is linked (DBS yuu cl.11, cl.17);
-- the HSBC Everyday Global Account deposits ADB, which selects 9 or 19 bonus
-- points (HSBC cl.7.1(ii)); PAssion membership and linkage (PAssion cl.1).
--
-- NOT MODELLED, AND WHY. The household cheat sheet credits Solitaire with
-- "6 mpd (4 card + 2 savings)" conditional on a Lady's Savings Account
-- balance. The UOB Lady's Cards T&C in this corpus states 10X UNI$ per S$5 =
-- 4 miles per S$1 and says nothing about a savings-account uplift; that
-- promotion's terms are NOT HELD. The uplift is therefore Unconfirmed in
-- every answer, not a field here.
DECLARE Cardholder
    HAS `Solitaire preferred rewards categories`                 IS A LIST OF `Solitaire category`
        `yuu account linked to the card`                         IS A BOOLEAN
        `HSBC Everyday Global Account deposits ADB this month`   IS A NUMBER
        `PAssion membership registered and card linked in yuu`   IS A BOOLEAN


§§ `Where the month stands`

-- The monthly thresholds the T&Cs condition bonus rates on, as at the
-- moment of the transaction. These are FACTS about the month so far (a
-- statement shows them); whether they satisfy a threshold is each card
-- module's rule. Cap ROOM is deliberately not here: how much of a cap
-- remains is the allocation problem, out of scope (spec section 6).
--
-- yuu cl.7(b): "Minimum S$800 worth of Eligible Spend ... and Transact at 4
-- Participating Merchants" per calendar month. PAssion cl.8(a): "At least
-- S$300 total spend at Participating Stores" and "At least S$400 total spend
-- at non-Participating Stores" per Qualifying Month.
DECLARE `Month position`
    HAS `yuu eligible spend so far this calendar month`                     IS A NUMBER
        `yuu participating merchants transacted so far this calendar month` IS A NUMBER
        `PAssion spend at Participating Stores so far this month`           IS A NUMBER
        `PAssion spend at non-Participating Stores so far this month`       IS A NUMBER


§§ `Provenance and epistemic status`

-- Every rule answers with its sources attached (spec section 8). A
-- Provenance names the document by its id in source/source-bundle.json, the
-- clause as the issuer numbers it, the document's own dating line, and the
-- operative words verbatim.
DECLARE Provenance
    HAS `document`   IS A STRING
        `clause`     IS A STRING
        `dated`      IS A STRING
        `document date`  IS A DATE
        `quote`      IS A STRING

-- `document date` is the same fact as `dated`, as a DATE: the day the
-- document says it was updated or took effect, so that two citations can be
-- compared for currency. (It also keeps this record from being all-string,
-- which the Catala exporter could not emit; measured 2026-09-21.)

-- What a reader may do with the answer.
--   Verified          -- read from a held T&C, clause cited
--   Unconfirmed       -- the T&C that would settle it is not held, or is
--                        silent; a real value that propagates, never a default
--   `Source expired`  -- read from a held T&C whose own stated period has
--                        ended (PAssion cl.3: 1 Oct 2023 to 30 Sep 2025)
--   `Disputed by source` -- a secondary source claims otherwise and the
--                        primary text does not support it
DECLARE Status IS ONE OF
    Verified
    Unconfirmed
    `Source expired`
    `Disputed by source`

-- The basis on which a monthly cap refills, and the date a purchase is
-- attributed by. Two independent axes (spec section 4.3): settling one does
-- not settle the other. `Unconfirmed` is a value on both.
DECLARE `Cap basis` IS ONE OF
    `Calendar month`
    `Statement month`
    `Unconfirmed basis`

DECLARE Attribution IS ONE OF
    `By transaction date`
    `By posting date`
    `Unconfirmed attribution`

-- A monthly bonus cap as the T&C states it, converted to the dollars of
-- bonus-earning spend it admits. `capped` FALSE means this transaction does
-- not run against any pool, whether because the T&C states no cap or because
-- the cap does not reach this transaction (PAssion and HSBC answer FALSE on
-- documents that do state a cap), and the dollar figure is meaningless. `pool` names WHICH pot the
-- transaction runs against, because one card may keep several (UOB cl.6(b):
-- each Preferred Rewards Category capped separately; HSBC cl.7.2: one pool
-- for contactless and online together) and the cheat sheet's claim that two
-- pots do not transfer is only checkable if the pot is named.
DECLARE Cap
    HAS `pool`                         IS A STRING
        `capped`                       IS A BOOLEAN
        `bonus spend per month`        IS A NUMBER
        `basis`                        IS A `Cap basis`
        `attribution`                  IS AN Attribution


§§ `The answer`

-- What every card module returns for (cardholder, month position,
-- transaction): the rate with its conditions, its citations and its status.
-- When the merchant indicator is Unknown and the card's rate turns on it,
-- the two conditional rates differ, `depends on the merchant indicator` is
-- TRUE, and `miles per dollar` carries the LOWER branch so the headline
-- figure never overstates (spec section 7: the projection must render the
-- uncertainty, not resolve it).
-- `points per dollar` is the issuer's own unit (yuu Points, DBS Points, UNI$,
-- HSBC Reward points, Citi ThankYou Points) and is what the T&C states;
-- `miles per point` is the conversion, and `conversion status` says whether
-- a HELD document states it (yuu cl.12: "0.28 miles or more"; UOB cl.3:
-- "UNI$1 = 2 miles") or not (HSBC and Citi state their conversions in
-- programme documents this corpus does not hold).
DECLARE `Rate answer`
    HAS `points per dollar`                                   IS A NUMBER
        `miles per point`                                     IS A NUMBER
        `conversion status`                                   IS A Status
        `miles per dollar`                                    IS A NUMBER
        `miles per dollar if the merchant flags online`       IS A NUMBER
        `miles per dollar if the merchant flags card-present` IS A NUMBER
        `depends on the merchant indicator`                   IS A BOOLEAN
        `earns the bonus rate`                                IS A BOOLEAN
        `cap`                                                 IS A Cap
        `conditions`                                          IS A LIST OF STRING
        `sources`                                             IS A LIST OF Provenance
        `status`                                              IS A Status
